\begin{align*}
\text{Angle }A + \text{Angle }B &= 180 \\[1ex]
\end{align*}

We're also told that angle A is 8 times the measure of angle B, so we can write:

\begin{align*}
\text{Angle }A &= 8 \times \text{Angle }B \\[1ex]
\end{align*}

Now we can substitute the expression for angle A from the second equation into the first equation:

\begin{align*}
8 \times \text{Angle }B + \text{Angle }B &= 180\\
(8 + 1) \times \text{Angle }B &= 180\\
9 \times \text{Angle }B &= 180
\end{align*}

To find the measure of angle B, we divide both sides by 9:

\begin{align*}
\text{Angle }B &= \frac{180}{9} \\
&= 20
\end{align*}

Now that we have the measure of angle B, we can find the measure of angle A using the second equation:

\begin{align*}
\text{Angle }A &= 8 \times \text{Angle }B \\
&= 8 \times 20 \\
&= 160
\end{align*}

